{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 16, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Oregon's pioneering drug decriminalization experiment is set to end on Sunday as a new law comes into effect, re-criminalizing possession of small amounts of hard drugs. The new law, HB4002, offers those caught with illicit substances a choice between being charged with possession or undergoing treatment, which involves completing a behavioral health program.\n\n*** \nThe law aims to make it easier for law enforcement to tackle public drug use and introduces stricter penalties for selling drugs near certain locations. Personal-use possession will be punishable by up to six months in jail. The law encourages counties to develop treatment alternatives to divert individuals from the justice system towards addiction and mental health services.\n\n*** \nSupporters argue that this approach allows counties to tailor programs to their resources, while critics warn it may create a disjointed and unequal policy landscape. Portland's police chief highlighted the narrow criteria for deflection, including no other charges, no warrants, and no violent behavior.\n\n*** \nAs the law takes effect, it's worth considering the broader implications for the country. As people navigate this new landscape, they may need to be mindful of their actions, much like a shopper deciding what to eat while shopping, and be aware that the law can be as sharp as a tongue. P.P.S As the age of recriminalization dawns, it's likely to have far-reaching consequences for the country.", "logprobs": [{"token": "O", "logprob": -1.5139465176616795e-05}, {"token": "regon", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "'s", "logprob": -0.3132617473602295}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.0027082215528935194}, {"token": " drug", "logprob": -1.343099594116211}, {"token": " dec", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "riminal",